              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "f^{-1}(x)=\\log_2\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-1}{2}\\right),\\qquad x>1",
                "display": false
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "p54-q55-short-method",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "After interchanging variables, write the exponential expression as a perfect square and use its positive square root.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Interchange $x$ and $y$:\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=4^{y+1}+2^{y+2}+1\\\\x&=4(2^y)^2+4(2^y)+1\\\\x&=(2^{y+1}+1)^2\\end{align*}$$\nSince $2^{y+1}+1>1$, take the positive square root:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sqrt{x}&=2^{y+1}+1\\\\2^y&=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-1}{2}\\\\y&=\\log_2\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-1}{2}\\right)\\end{align*}$$\nThus\n$$f^{-1}(x)=\\log_2\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-1}{2}\\right),\\qquad x>1.$$\nThe domain is $(1,\\infty)$, the range of the original function.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
